+---
+name: jenkins-pipelines
+description: |
+  The estate's Jenkins pipeline style guide at https://pipelines.home/docs/ —
+  its hard rules by id, and where the guide details each. Use whenever writing,
+  editing or reviewing a Jenkinsfile (`Jenkinsfile` or `Jenkinsfile.<purpose>`),
+  creating a Jenkins job, or deciding whether code goes into the
+  JenkinsPipelineUtils library.
+---
+
+Every Jenkinsfile in the estate follows the style guide at
+https://pipelines.home/docs/, strictly: a file that breaks one of its rules is
+wrong, whatever else it gets right. The guide is declarative only, and it is the
+only source. This skill names its hard rules in brief, each by its id, so you
+know what to hold a file to; the guide's page holds the rule's exact wording,
+its reason and a recipe. Read a rule's page before you write a file to it.
+
+**Reading the guide.** https://pipelines.home/docs/llms.txt indexes every page
+as a Markdown copy, and `llms-full.txt` beside it holds the whole site in one
+file. A page's Markdown copy is `https://pipelines.home/docs/<path>/index.md`,
+and a rule's id stands in its heading (`POD-4`); a browser reads the page at
+`…/<path>/` and the rule at `#pod-4`. Each library var has its reference page at
+`https://pipelines.home/docs/reference/<var>/index.md`.
+
+**Start from the type's reference file.** Every pipeline type has a complete
+reference Jenkinsfile that applies every rule, on its page at
+`https://pipelines.home/docs/types/<stem>/`. The overview,
+https://pipelines.home/docs/types/index.md, names the types and which job each
+reference file comes from. A new pipeline starts from its type's reference file.
+
+## The hard rules
+
+### [Files and headers](https://pipelines.home/docs/guide/file-layout/index.md)
+
+- One declarative `pipeline {}`; no scripted `node`, `podTemplate` or `stage`
+  outside it (FILE-1).
+- Named `Jenkinsfile` or `Jenkinsfile.<purpose>`, at the repo root;
+  `Jenkinsfile.architecture` is the architecture producer (FILE-2).
+- A header comment first: what the job does and every change it makes outside
+  the build, ending in a `Controller config:` block — `Job:`, `SCM:`,
+  `Script Path:`, never triggers or concurrency (FILE-3). No link to the guide
+  (FILE-4).
+- The library line, once and exactly:
+  `library identifier: 'JenkinsPipelineUtils', changelog: false` — no
+  `@Library`, no version (FILE-5).
+- Header, import, library line, local code, `pipeline {}`, and nothing after it
+  (FILE-6). A comment says why, never what the next line does (FILE-7).
+
+### [Job properties](https://pipelines.home/docs/guide/job-properties/index.md)
+
+- Blocks in order: `agent`, `options`, `triggers`, `parameters`, `stages`,
+  `post` (PROP-1).
+- `options {}` holds, in order and nothing else: `disableConcurrentBuilds(…)`,
+  `skipDefaultCheckout()`, `timeout(…)`, `timestamps()`, and
+  `copyArtifactPermission('<job>')` only where another job copies this job's
+  artifacts (PROP-2, PROP-4).
+- `disableConcurrentBuilds(abortPrevious: true)`. Plain
+  `disableConcurrentBuilds()`, with a comment saying why, only for a build that
+  decides from its change sets what to build or makes a sequence of changes an
+  abort would cut in half; the guide lists those jobs (PROP-3).
+- No `buildDiscarder` (PROP-5). Built on push: `triggers { githubPush() }`;
+  scheduled: `triggers { cron('…') }` with `H` in the minute field; started by
+  hand: no `triggers {}` (PROP-6). Parameters in `parameters {}` (PROP-7). No
+  `properties` step, except AaC/Architecture's `Set triggers` (PROP-8).
+
+### [Checkout](https://pipelines.home/docs/guide/checkout/index.md)
+
+- `skipDefaultCheckout()`, and the first stage is `Checkout`, running
+  `checkout scm`; the job's own repo is never cloned with `git` or in a shell
+  (CHK-1). The iac-controller files too (CHK-4).
+- Other repos the build reads are cloned in that same stage, each in
+  `dir('<Repo>')`, the job's own included (CHK-2).
+- A push never goes from the `Checkout` stage's clone: the stage that pushes
+  clones the repo inside `withCredentials` and pushes from that clone, the job's
+  own repo included. A pin write clones nothing (CHK-3).
+
+### [Agent and pod](https://pipelines.home/docs/guide/pod/index.md)
+
+- One `agent { kubernetes { … } }`, at the top of `pipeline {}`; no stage
+  declares an agent (POD-1). An iac-controller job:
+  `agent { label 'iac-controller' }` (POD-2).
+- `inheritFrom 'jenkins-agent'`, or `jenkins-agent-large` in the jobs the guide
+  lists. `kaniko` only for a build that runs kaniko, and then with
+  `yamlMergeStrategy merge()` (POD-3).
+- Every other container comes from
+  `yaml podYaml(templates: [...], images: [...])`. An `images:` entry is a map
+  with an explicit `name:`, `[image: 'espressif/idf:v5.5.3', name: 'idf']`,
+  never a string (POD-4). A library sidecar is named in `templates:`, never
+  under `images:` (POD-5). No container that no step runs in (POD-6).
+
+### [Secrets and settings](https://pipelines.home/docs/guide/secrets/index.md)
+
+- `withVault` goes inside the `steps` of the stage that uses the secret, around
+  only the steps that use it; never around `pipeline {}`, the agent, the
+  checkout, a lint or a test (SEC-1). No secret in the pod spec (SEC-2).
+- The one Jenkins credential is GitHub's,
+  `5f6fbd66-b41c-405f-b107-85ba6fd97f10`. A shell that pushes gets it from
+  `withCredentials([usernamePassword(…)])` around that shell only (SEC-3).
+- A shell reads a secret from its environment (`\$TOKEN` in a `"""` script); a
+  secret is never interpolated into a Groovy string (SEC-4).
+- A setting that is not a secret is written in the file where it is used. No
+  controller global environment variable except `HA_URL`, and no URL or host
+  from OpenBao (SEC-5).
+
+### [Timeouts](https://pipelines.home/docs/guide/timeouts/index.md)
+
+- A pod pipeline: `timeout(time: 60, unit: 'MINUTES')` in its pipeline-level
+  `options {}` (TIME-1). An iac-controller file:
+  `timeout(time: 4, unit: 'HOURS')` (TIME-2). The one exception is DockerImages,
+  180 minutes (TIME-3).
+- A `timeout` step inside a stage bounds a step known to hang, under a comment
+  naming the hang, and never sits inside `catchError` (TIME-4). An iac dev stage
+  bounds its playbook with the shell's `timeout --kill-after=1m <n>m` (TIME-5).
+
+### [Stage granularity](https://pipelines.home/docs/guide/stage-granularity/index.md)
+
+- A stage for each outcome, and no other stage: the checkout, first; one stage
+  per gate; one per artifact; one per change outside the build, such as a pin
+  write, an upload, an apply, a push or a started job (GRAN-1 to GRAN-5). Never
+  split to have more stages, never merged to have fewer.
+- A change comes after the gates and builds it depends on (GRAN-6).
+- Every stage is written out as `stage('<label>')`; only DockerImages generates
+  stages (GRAN-7). A stage that runs only sometimes says so in `when {}`
+  (GRAN-8).
+
+### [Stage labels](https://pipelines.home/docs/guide/stage-labels/index.md)
+
+- An imperative verb, then its object, in sentence case: `Build firmware`. A
+  name is spelled as it is elsewhere (LABEL-1).
+- The standard labels, where they apply: `Checkout`, `Lint`, `Test`,
+  `Validate <artifact>`, `Build <image> image`, `Build <artifact>`,
+  `Generate <artifact>`, `Write image pins`, `Trigger <job>`, `Deploy firmware`
+  (LABEL-2).
+- The scope last, in parentheses: `Apply Terraform (prd)` (LABEL-3). Never `+`:
+  `Lint and test` (LABEL-4). Never the repo or job name, nor anything the stage
+  does not do (LABEL-5). Unique within the file, and a string literal (LABEL-6).
+
+### [post and notify](https://pipelines.home/docs/guide/post-and-notify/index.md)
+
+- `notify` only for what the build's result does not say, never for a failure
+  (POST-1). A failed iac dev stage calls `notify.warning(message)`, then
+  `unstable(message)`, in the stage (POST-2).
+- A file with plain `disableConcurrentBuilds()` ends with the guide's abort
+  marker `post` block; a file with `abortPrevious: true` has no
+  `post { aborted }` (POST-3). `post` holds only alerts and cleanup (POST-4).
+
+### [The library](https://pipelines.home/docs/guide/library/index.md)
+
+- Code goes into JenkinsPipelineUtils when it carries a contract that must be
+  the same across the estate; a repo's own workflow stays in its files, however
+  many copies there are (LIB-1). The library never takes over the job's
+  properties, trigger or agent size (LIB-2). A per-repo value is an argument
+  with no default (LIB-3). A whole-pipeline helper needs three jobs whose files
+  are one body (LIB-4). A new var comes with its page, `vars/<name>.md`, in the
+  same commit (LIB-5).
+- An image build calls `helmCharts.kaniko2(…)`, never the positional
+  `helmCharts.kaniko(…)` (LIB-6). Each iac-controller file carries its own
+  dev-stage code (LIB-7). Logic only one file uses is a `def` before
+  `pipeline {}`, `@NonCPS` only when it is pure and calls no step (LIB-8).
+
+### [A new repo](https://pipelines.home/docs/guide/new-repo/index.md)
+
+- A new job is created through the Jenkins API, from the guide's `config.xml`
+  template (NEW-1). A push-built job's `config.xml` carries `GitHubPushTrigger`
+  when the job is created: the file's `triggers {}` never install the repo's
+  push hook (NEW-2). A new job goes into its product's folder, and an existing
+  job is never moved (NEW-3).
